{
  "evidence": "TEXT_E: NHS investment to triple with 18‑week referral-to-treatment maximum; public health measures including smoke‑free workplaces/restaurants, restrictions on junk‑food marketing to children, better school meals, and expanded cessation services; commitment to halve child poverty by 2010 and end it in a generation; universal childcare via Sure Start and extended schools; higher minimum wage, tax credits, Pension Credit, Winter Fuel Payments, and free local bus travel; strong environmental goals (CO2 cuts −20% by 2010, −60% by 2050); Single Equality Act and an Equality & Human Rights Commission; neighbourhood policing and youth justice reforms. TEXT_G: Emphasis on leaving the EU to restore sovereignty; near‑zero net migration with Britishness tests; stronger magistrates, more prison capacity, and tougher sentencing; NHS changes focused on local control and removing EU limits; tax cuts (e.g., halving council tax, flat tax consideration) and deregulation; ban GM crops until proven safe and protect the green belt; uphold Parliament/monarchy, repeal the Human Rights Act, oppose ID cards.",
  "reasoning": "The electorate weights care most highly, and TEXT_E foregrounds care/harm through large NHS investment, child‑poverty reduction, universal childcare, public health protections, and support for the vulnerable, while also offering moderate authority and equality commitments. TEXT_G leans more on loyalty/authority via sovereignty and immigration controls and offers less extensive care/equality provisions, making it a weaker fit for a care‑dominant profile.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
